# .worktreeinclude — gantry copies/clones untracked paths matching these
# patterns into every new worktree it creates. A fresh `git worktree add`
# checks out tracked files only, so anything gitignored — env files,
# installed dependencies — would otherwise be missing from a new workspace.
#
# Syntax is real .gitignore syntax (matched via the `ignore` crate, the same
# engine ripgrep uses): patterns, `!` negation, `#` comments.
#
# Two mechanisms, picked by what a pattern matches:
#   - A pattern matching a FILE is copied (bytes only, never symlinked — a
#     symlinked .env would leak one workspace's edits into every other,
#     defeating the isolation worktrees exist for).
#   - A pattern matching a DIRECTORY is cloned whole via APFS clonefile
#     (copy-on-write: near-instant, ~zero space until the two trees
#     diverge). A non-APFS volume silently skips the directory rather than
#     failing worktree creation.
#
# A path that's already tracked by git is skipped either way — the checkout
# already placed it, and copying again could overwrite it with a stale copy
# from main's current working tree.
#
# Uncomment whatever this project actually needs:

# .env
# .env.local
# node_modules/
# target/
# .venv/
